# Copy to infra/.env and fill in real values before `docker compose up`.
# See infra/README.md for the full walkthrough of what each piece is and
# the order to bring them up in.

# ---- bootstrap-node -------------------------------------------------
# Public host/IP this node is reachable at — used only to construct the
# multiaddr you'll hand out to clients after the container starts (see
# README "Publishing the bootstrap address"). Not read by the daemon
# itself.
BOOTSTRAP_PUBLIC_HOST=bootstrap1.example.org
BOOTSTRAP_PUBLIC_PORT=4001

# ---- push-relay -------------------------------------------------------
PUSH_RELAY_DOMAIN=push-relay.example.org
PUSH_RELAY_PORT=47900

# ---- coturn -------------------------------------------------------
# Two mutually exclusive credential schemes — set one, not both (if both
# are set, TURN_AUTH_SECRET wins, matching
# bh-calls::transport::default_ice_servers's own priority order):
#
# Option A — TURN_AUTH_SECRET (recommended): coturn's `use-auth-secret`/
# TURN REST API scheme. The shared secret below never goes out over the
# wire; every daemon that also has this same secret set derives a fresh,
# time-limited username/credential pair per call
# (bh-calls::transport::ephemeral_turn_credential, HMAC-SHA1 per coturn's
# own spec). A leaked derived credential expires on its own
# (BLACKHOLE_TURN_CREDENTIAL_TTL_SECS on the daemon side, default 24h);
# only the shared secret itself is permanent, and it's never transmitted.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
TURN_AUTH_SECRET=change-me-to-a-real-random-secret
#
# Option B — TURN_USERNAME/TURN_CREDENTIAL: one fixed long-term-credential
# pair (coturn's `lt-cred-mech`). Simpler, but this exact credential is
# what every daemon presents on every call, forever, until an operator
# rotates it and restarts every daemon by hand.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
TURN_USERNAME=blackhole
TURN_CREDENTIAL=change-me-to-a-real-random-secret
#
TURN_REALM=turn.example.org
# Public IP of the coturn host — coturn needs this to hand out correct
# relay candidates to clients behind NAT (its `external-ip` setting).
TURN_PUBLIC_IP=203.0.113.10
